## Test Organization

### File Structure
```
currency/
  pairs_test.go
arbitrage/
  arbitrage_test.go
  orderbook_test.go
utils/
  config_test.go
```

### Test Naming
- Use descriptive test names
- Include the scenario being tested
- Use table-driven tests for multiple cases
- Group related tests in subtests

### Test Helpers
```go
func createTestTriangle() *arbitrage.Triangle {
    return &arbitrage.Triangle{
        PathA: &arbitrage.Path{
            Pair:      currency.Pair{Base: currency.USDC, Quote: currency.BTC},
            Direction: "BUY",
        },
        // ... other paths
    }
}

func assertProfitWithinRange(t *testing.T, expected, actual float64, tolerance float64) {
    if math.Abs(expected-actual) > tolerance {
        t.Errorf("Expected profit %f, got %f (tolerance: %f)", expected, actual, tolerance)
    }
}
```

## Continuous Integration

### Automated Testing
- Run tests on every commit
- Include simulation mode tests
- Validate configuration loading
- Test with different Go versions

### Test Coverage
- Aim for high test coverage on critical paths
- Focus on trading logic and calculations
- Include error handling paths
- Test configuration and validation

### Pre-commit Hooks
- Run tests before allowing commits
- Validate code formatting
- Check for common issues
- Ensure simulation mode is default

## Production Readiness

### Simulation Validation
- Run extended simulation periods
- Validate against historical data
- Test with real market conditions
- Monitor performance metrics

### Gradual Rollout
- Start with very small amounts
- Monitor all trades closely
- Implement additional safety checks
- Have rollback procedures ready

### Monitoring and Alerting
- Set up alerts for simulation vs live mode
- Monitor error rates and patterns
- Track performance metrics
- Implement health checks